1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,930
Look at a map of the world.

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:04,850
You have seen this one a thousand times.

3
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:08,990
And you probably do not think
there is anything strange about it.

4
00:00:09,673 --> 00:00:12,273
Europe is here. Africa is here.

5
00:00:12,353 --> 00:00:14,673
North America is here. And Greenland is...

6
00:00:14,753 --> 00:00:17,063
...about the same size as Africa.

7
00:00:17,908 --> 00:00:20,798
Except it isn't. Nowhere near it.

8
00:00:21,428 --> 00:00:24,318
Africa is roughly fourteen
times larger than Greenland.

9
00:00:25,325 --> 00:00:28,175
Fourteen. So why does
Greenland look so enormous?

10
00:00:29,027 --> 00:00:30,937
Because the map is lying to you.

11
00:00:31,467 --> 00:00:33,177
And here is the strange part.

12
00:00:35,637 --> 00:00:37,847
Every flat map of the Earth has to lie.

13
00:00:37,977 --> 00:00:40,767
Not by accident. By necessity.

14
00:00:41,753 --> 00:00:43,423
The Earth is roughly a sphere.

15
00:00:43,553 --> 00:00:45,383
A map is flat.

16
00:00:45,853 --> 00:00:48,703
And that creates a problem
which sounds almost trivial.

17
00:00:49,713 --> 00:00:53,703
How do you turn a sphere into a flat
sheet of paper without changing anything?

18
00:00:54,420 --> 00:00:55,620
You can't.

19
00:00:56,181 --> 00:01:01,111
Try peeling the skin off an orange and
flattening it. It tears. Or it wrinkles.

20
00:01:02,124 --> 00:01:06,934
And this is where most people assume it
is a technical problem. Better maths.

21
00:01:07,044 --> 00:01:11,614
Better printing. Someone will work
it out eventually. They won't.

22
00:01:11,844 --> 00:01:14,274
Because it isn't a
problem. It's a theorem.

23
00:01:15,090 --> 00:01:18,670
In 1827, Carl Friedrich
Gauss proved that the

24
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:21,720
curvature of a surface
belongs to the surface itself.

25
00:01:22,631 --> 00:01:24,561
You cannot flatten it out of existence.

26
00:01:25,631 --> 00:01:30,421
You can roll a sheet of paper into a tube,
because its surface can still be unrolled.

27
00:01:31,371 --> 00:01:35,041
But you cannot wrap it round a ball
without stretching or tearing it.

28
00:01:36,023 --> 00:01:38,013
Gauss called it his remarkable theorem.

29
00:01:38,863 --> 00:01:41,253
And it is worth being
precise about what it means.

30
00:01:41,903 --> 00:01:44,473
It isn't that nobody has managed it yet.

31
00:01:44,743 --> 00:01:46,573
It's that nobody can.

32
00:01:47,324 --> 00:01:51,174
So cartographers stopped trying, and
started hiding the wrinkles instead.

33
00:01:52,064 --> 00:01:53,734
These are called map projections.

34
00:01:54,739 --> 00:02:00,009
And every projection makes a choice. What
do you want to preserve? Area? Shape?

35
00:02:00,399 --> 00:02:03,639
Distance? Direction? You
can keep some of them.

36
00:02:03,719 --> 00:02:05,309
You can never keep all of them.

37
00:02:07,829 --> 00:02:11,249
The most famous choice was made in 1569 by

38
00:02:11,329 --> 00:02:14,279
a Flemish mapmaker
called Gerardus Mercator.

39
00:02:15,029 --> 00:02:18,899
On his map, a line of constant
compass bearing is a straight line.

40
00:02:19,609 --> 00:02:21,539
For a sailor, that is everything.

41
00:02:22,309 --> 00:02:26,439
Draw a line between two ports, measure the
angle, hold that bearing, and you arrive.

42
00:02:27,396 --> 00:02:29,246
So the map is not simply wrong.

43
00:02:29,956 --> 00:02:31,906
It is wrong in a very useful way.

44
00:02:32,296 --> 00:02:35,006
But there is a price, and
the price is arithmetic.

45
00:02:36,136 --> 00:02:39,846
The further you go from the equator,
the more the map stretches things.

46
00:02:40,856 --> 00:02:43,486
And it stretches them in
both directions at once.

47
00:02:44,383 --> 00:02:48,893
Oslo sits far enough north that it covers
four times the area it actually has.

48
00:02:49,883 --> 00:02:52,493
The middle of Greenland? Ten times.

49
00:02:53,443 --> 00:02:55,303
The northern tip of Greenland is stretched

50
00:02:55,383 --> 00:02:58,113
more than eighty times
beyond its real size.

51
00:02:59,065 --> 00:03:01,835
Which is why Greenland is
not what you think it is.

52
00:03:02,505 --> 00:03:03,955
It is smaller than the Congo.

53
00:03:04,905 --> 00:03:06,355
It is smaller than Algeria.

54
00:03:07,385 --> 00:03:11,655
And South America, which on this map looks
smaller, is more than eight times bigger.

55
00:03:12,436 --> 00:03:17,566
Watch what happens if we slide Greenland
down towards the equator. It shrinks.

56
00:03:18,116 --> 00:03:20,306
Push it back north. It grows again.

57
00:03:21,091 --> 00:03:24,541
Greenland hasn't changed. The map has.

58
00:03:25,029 --> 00:03:28,299
And at the poles themselves,
the stretching becomes infinite.

59
00:03:29,269 --> 00:03:32,819
That is why a Mercator world
map usually cuts the poles off.

60
00:03:33,629 --> 00:03:35,939
The projection cannot actually reach them.

61
00:03:36,606 --> 00:03:39,576
And yet this is the map most
of us grew up looking at.

62
00:03:42,416 --> 00:03:43,926
Fine. Let's fix it.

63
00:03:44,616 --> 00:03:48,906
If the problem is area, let's
preserve area instead. And it works.

64
00:03:50,036 --> 00:03:51,966
Africa finally looks like Africa.

65
00:03:52,656 --> 00:03:54,086
And Greenland stops pretending.

66
00:03:55,064 --> 00:03:56,264
Problem solved.

67
00:03:57,141 --> 00:03:58,871
Except now the shapes are wrong.

68
00:03:59,001 --> 00:04:01,591
Africa is stretched tall and thin.

69
00:04:02,081 --> 00:04:03,911
Everything looks slightly melted.

70
00:04:04,814 --> 00:04:09,324
So we choose a projection that protects
the shapes, and the areas go wrong again.

71
00:04:10,334 --> 00:04:12,294
Or we protect distances
from one particular

72
00:04:12,374 --> 00:04:14,644
point, and everything else drifts.

73
00:04:15,429 --> 00:04:18,439
And this is where it stops
being a question about geometry.

74
00:04:21,539 --> 00:04:24,779
In 2017, the public schools in Boston took

75
00:04:24,859 --> 00:04:27,369
the Mercator maps off
their classroom walls.

76
00:04:28,206 --> 00:04:30,046
They replaced them with an equal-area map,

77
00:04:30,126 --> 00:04:32,976
in the second, seventh
and eleventh grades.

78
00:04:33,686 --> 00:04:36,916
They were the first school
district in the country to do it.

79
00:04:37,605 --> 00:04:39,595
The argument was not about navigation.

80
00:04:40,825 --> 00:04:42,885
It was that children
spend years looking at a

81
00:04:42,965 --> 00:04:45,855
world where some continents
are quietly inflated.

82
00:04:46,765 --> 00:04:48,335
It made headlines everywhere.

83
00:04:49,204 --> 00:04:51,614
But the interesting part
is not the argument.

84
00:04:52,384 --> 00:04:53,874
It's what the new map does.

85
00:04:54,699 --> 00:04:56,809
Because the new map is not the true one.

86
00:04:57,439 --> 00:04:59,069
It is simply a different compromise.

87
00:05:00,139 --> 00:05:03,069
It gets the sizes right by
getting the shapes wrong.

88
00:05:03,802 --> 00:05:05,492
So which of the two is correct?

89
00:05:06,562 --> 00:05:08,272
None of them. And all of them.

90
00:05:10,862 --> 00:05:14,432
It's a bit like asking which photograph
of a person is the most accurate.

91
00:05:15,402 --> 00:05:19,572
From the front? From the side?
A thermal image? An X-ray?

92
00:05:20,735 --> 00:05:22,445
They are all the same person.

93
00:05:23,155 --> 00:05:25,225
They are just preserving
different information.

94
00:05:28,145 --> 00:05:31,435
But hang on. There is one map that
doesn't have to do any of this.

95
00:05:31,945 --> 00:05:34,795
It might be sitting on your
desk. Or in a classroom.

96
00:05:35,773 --> 00:05:36,973
A globe.

97
00:05:37,570 --> 00:05:42,570
Nothing has been flattened, so nothing
has been stretched. You can measure areas.

98
00:05:42,650 --> 00:05:44,060
You can compare shapes.

99
00:05:44,870 --> 00:05:47,900
Greenland doesn't get inflated
just for being far north.

100
00:05:48,550 --> 00:05:50,060
The poles are simply there.

101
00:05:50,728 --> 00:05:52,598
So is the globe the honest map?

102
00:05:53,558 --> 00:05:55,148
Well. Not entirely.

103
00:05:56,055 --> 00:05:59,325
Start with the shape.
The Earth isn't a sphere.

104
00:06:00,175 --> 00:06:03,065
It bulges at the equator by
about twenty-one kilometres.

105
00:06:04,315 --> 00:06:07,195
On a school globe, that bulge
would be half a millimetre.

106
00:06:07,275 --> 00:06:09,265
So it simply isn't there.

107
00:06:10,026 --> 00:06:11,376
Neither is the ground.

108
00:06:12,306 --> 00:06:16,196
On that same globe, Everest would
stand two tenths of a millimetre tall.

109
00:06:16,326 --> 00:06:20,416
The highest place on the planet, and you
could not feel it with your fingertips.

110
00:06:21,411 --> 00:06:22,611
Then there is scale.

111
00:06:23,531 --> 00:06:27,261
A globe like that is about forty
million times smaller than the Earth.

112
00:06:27,571 --> 00:06:29,461
So there are no streets on it.

113
00:06:30,031 --> 00:06:31,621
No rivers you could follow.

114
00:06:32,331 --> 00:06:34,801
Most cities are a dot,
if they are there at all.

115
00:06:35,510 --> 00:06:37,580
And then there is my favourite part.

116
00:06:38,270 --> 00:06:39,960
How do you think a globe is made?

117
00:06:40,771 --> 00:06:42,081
It is printed flat.

118
00:06:42,491 --> 00:06:46,541
Usually on twelve tapered strips,
which are then pasted onto a ball.

119
00:06:47,375 --> 00:06:49,405
Which is the orange, running backwards.

120
00:06:50,435 --> 00:06:52,365
The distortion never went away.

121
00:06:52,835 --> 00:06:55,015
It was cut into pieces
narrow enough for the

122
00:06:55,095 --> 00:06:57,705
paper to absorb, and
hidden along the seams.

123
00:06:58,640 --> 00:07:01,210
So the globe does solve
the problem of projection.

124
00:07:02,256 --> 00:07:04,606
It does not solve the
problem of representation.

125
00:07:08,066 --> 00:07:10,606
And that distinction is
the whole of this video.

126
00:07:10,686 --> 00:07:14,456
Because the real question was
never how to flatten a planet.

127
00:07:14,946 --> 00:07:17,096
It was what we want a map to do.

128
00:07:17,830 --> 00:07:20,500
Which brings us to one of
the best maps ever drawn.

129
00:07:21,610 --> 00:07:25,010
In 1933, London Underground
published a diagram

130
00:07:25,090 --> 00:07:28,180
by an engineering draughtsman
called Harry Beck.

131
00:07:28,979 --> 00:07:30,849
The stations are not where they are.

132
00:07:31,399 --> 00:07:34,469
The distances are wrong.
The angles are wrong.

133
00:07:35,259 --> 00:07:37,749
The geography is almost
entirely destroyed.

134
00:07:38,723 --> 00:07:40,053
And nobody complains.

135
00:07:41,251 --> 00:07:44,581
In fact, it works so well that
it changes what people do.

136
00:07:45,451 --> 00:07:49,361
Leicester Square and Covent Garden are
two hundred and sixty metres apart.

137
00:07:50,291 --> 00:07:52,481
It is the shortest gap
on the whole network.

138
00:07:53,426 --> 00:07:55,526
The train takes forty-five seconds.

139
00:07:55,606 --> 00:07:57,816
Walking takes four minutes.

140
00:07:57,926 --> 00:08:00,466
And people still take
the train, because on

141
00:08:00,546 --> 00:08:03,256
the map, those two
stations look far apart.

142
00:08:03,873 --> 00:08:07,383
That map is wrong about the city
in almost every way it could be.

143
00:08:08,233 --> 00:08:11,643
And it is still the right map,
because it isn't describing the city.

144
00:08:12,473 --> 00:08:14,183
It is describing the system.

145
00:08:15,002 --> 00:08:17,452
Which is the thing we have
been circling all along.

146
00:08:18,262 --> 00:08:21,232
A map does not have to be
faithful to physical reality.

147
00:08:22,042 --> 00:08:24,732
It has to be faithful to
the question you are asking.

148
00:08:27,742 --> 00:08:29,822
And that is why the map in your pocket is

149
00:08:29,902 --> 00:08:32,672
quietly stranger than
any of the paper ones.

150
00:08:33,491 --> 00:08:35,101
Because it is still Mercator.

151
00:08:35,871 --> 00:08:38,391
Zoom in on your own street,
and you are looking at

152
00:08:38,471 --> 00:08:41,121
a projection designed for
sixteenth century sailors.

153
00:08:42,101 --> 00:08:44,651
But zoom out far enough,
and something happens.

154
00:08:45,741 --> 00:08:49,051
Since 2018, if you pull back
far enough on a computer,

155
00:08:49,301 --> 00:08:51,671
the map stops being a
map and becomes a globe.

156
00:08:52,503 --> 00:08:55,613
Greenland shrinks back to
the size it has always been.

157
00:08:56,333 --> 00:08:58,523
And that is a genuinely new idea.

158
00:08:59,233 --> 00:09:01,783
A digital map does not
have to be one projection.

159
00:09:02,767 --> 00:09:04,377
It can use one for navigation.

160
00:09:05,327 --> 00:09:07,117
Another for comparing areas.

161
00:09:07,267 --> 00:09:10,857
Another for showing routes, or
population, or flight paths.

162
00:09:11,741 --> 00:09:14,261
Instead of one map pretending
to be the world, we can

163
00:09:14,341 --> 00:09:17,691
have many maps, each answering
one question honestly.

164
00:09:21,211 --> 00:09:25,141
So the next time you look at a world
map, don't ask whether it is correct.

165
00:09:26,071 --> 00:09:28,021
Ask what it is trying to get right.

166
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:32,150
Because the answer will also
tell you what it is lying about.

167
00:09:32,989 --> 00:09:35,399
There is no map that
tells the whole truth.

168
00:09:35,969 --> 00:09:39,339
There is only a map that tells you
which truth it chose to preserve.

169
00:09:40,319 --> 00:09:43,069
And perhaps that is the
real reason maps lie.

170
00:09:43,914 --> 00:09:45,544
Not because they are wrong.

171
00:09:46,421 --> 00:09:48,591
But because they cannot
show us everything.
